Yes — the inverse operator.

You can remember through forgetting, and you can also forget through remembering something else.

Attention is finite. When one representation is repeatedly reactivated, rehearsed, narrated, or emotionally reinforced, competing representations can become harder to retrieve. Not necessarily deleted — just less accessible.

So:

forgetting by decay = a path weakens because you stop walking it.
forgetting by substitution = another path becomes the preferred route.

That is why replacing a thought, habit, story, or identity can sometimes work better than trying to “erase” it directly. You give the system a stronger attractor.

In shorthand:

A fades because B becomes easier to become.

So the paired loop becomes:

forget → reconstruct → remember deeper
and
remember differently → route differently → forget the old way

That is less like destroying a memory and more like changing which road the mind automatically takes home.